Plain `git` works as usual: GitHub SSH connections and commit signing reload
expired agent keys on their own. Once per session, or when Git or SSH fails,
inspect the repository-aware, non-secret projection:

```sh
braden-dots identity status --repo . --json
```

Use the reported SSH host for that repository. Account-specific identities use
aliases such as `github-<identity>`; do not guess the alias or rewrite a remote
to HTTPS to bypass routing. If the status reports `agent_keys_missing`, run
`braden-dots identity ensure` automatically and inspect status again.

On work machines, managed keys expire from the agent after nine hours. A push
or signed commit that still fails with `bad permissions` or
`Permission denied (publickey)` naming a `.pub` file means the agent lost its
keys and the automatic reload could not run. Run `braden-dots identity ensure`
and retry. Do not chmod the `.pub` files, rewrite the remote, or disable
commit signing: public keys are meant to be readable, and the private halves
exist only in the agent.

For GitHub API work, use the same repository-aware identity resolution instead
of ambient `gh` authentication:

```sh
braden-dots identity gh --repo . -- <gh arguments...>
```

This selects the default GitHub account unless the repository's generated
identity route selects a separate account. The verified token is cached for an
hour, so repeated calls are cheap; after `Bad credentials`, retry once with
`identity gh --repo . --refresh -- ...`.

The repository token cannot read check runs, so `gh pr checks` and
`statusCheckRollup` fail with `Resource not accessible by personal access
token`. Read CI through Actions instead: `gh run list --branch <branch>`,
`gh run view <run-id> --log-failed`, or
`gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/commits/<sha>/status`.

Use `braden-dots identity token-check --repo .` for non-mutating API credential
diagnostics. `provision-token` creates or authorizes account access and must
never be run by an agent without the user's explicit confirmation.

Ask for confirmation before authorizing an organisation through GitHub SAML
SSO. Never expose private keys, vault tokens, secret values, or raw sensitive
command output.

For requested changes to the Chezmoi source, preserve all existing dirty work
and use:

```sh
braden-dots source diff
braden-dots source publish -m "Describe the dotfiles change"
```

Never discard dirty source changes, auto-resolve semantic rebase conflicts,
force-push the dotfiles primary branch, or commit unrelated changes. When a
rebase conflicts, report the files and let the user choose or confirm the
semantic resolution before `braden-dots source continue`.
